package pages

// Field wrappers for the character sheet forms.
//
// The shape is DaisyUI's: .fieldset is the grid that stacks label / control /
// hint, .fieldset-legend is the label above it, .input is the control, and
// .validator wires the control's own constraint validation to both the border
// colour and the sibling .validator-hint. That replaces the IDLING -> ERROR
// state machine the lit-html components ran on blur; :user-invalid has the same
// semantics (it only matches once the user has interacted or submitted) and
// costs no JavaScript.
//
// .validator-hint keeps its line when there is no error to show: DaisyUI turns
// it off with `visibility`, not with `display`. So nothing jumps when one
// appears.
//
// The wrapper is a <div class="fieldset"> rather than a real <fieldset>. The
// original reason was normalize.css giving fieldset `padding: .35em .75em
// .625em` while DaisyUI's .fieldset only overrides the block axis, so a real
// one would inset the field from its grid cell. normalize.css is gone as of the
// cleanup pass and Tailwind's preflight zeroes fieldset padding, so that
// specific hazard has lifted -- but the div stays, because the semantic reason
// is the stronger one. A single labelled control is not a group -- the
// accessible name comes from <label for>, which is why the text above the field
// is a <label> carrying .fieldset-legend and not a <legend>.
//
// w-full is not optional: DaisyUI sizes .input at `clamp(3rem, 20rem, 100%)`,
// so a field in a cell wider than 20rem would otherwise stop short of it.

// .validator-hint is `visibility: hidden` in DaisyUI, which keeps the element in
// flow so nothing shifts when an error appears. That reserved line is 0.75rem of
// text plus a 0.5rem margin under *every* field, and stacked with the grid's own
// gap-4 it put roughly 2.6rem between form rows -- the sheet read as far more
// spaced out than it is dense.
//
// `hidden peer-user-invalid:block` collapses the element instead, so a row costs
// only what it displays. The trade is that a row grows when its error appears
// and pushes the rows below it down. That is the conventional behaviour and it
// only happens on an actual violation, which is worth more than permanently
// spending a line per field against it.
//
// DaisyUI's own `.validator:user-invalid ~ .validator-hint` rule still supplies
// the `visibility` flip and the error colour; only `display` is ours, which is
// why the input keeps `validator` and gains `peer` rather than swapping one for
// the other.
templ textField(name string, label string, value string, placeholder string, required bool) {
	<div class="fieldset">
		<label class="fieldset-legend" for={ name }>{ label }</label>
		<input
			id={ name }
			name={ name }
			type="text"
			class="input validator peer w-full"
			value={ value }
			placeholder={ placeholder }
			required?={ required }
		/>
		if required {
			<p class="validator-hint hidden peer-user-invalid:block text-hint">This field is required.</p>
		}
	</div>
}

// min/max/step carry over from number-input.js, which put min="0" max="9999"
// step="1" on every instance -- so negative values were already rejected before
// this migration. The hint has to cover the range violation as well as the
// empty-required one, which is why it is always rendered here and only rendered
// for required fields above.
templ numberField(name string, label string, value string, required bool) {
	<div class="fieldset">
		<label class="fieldset-legend" for={ name }>{ label }</label>
		<input
			id={ name }
			name={ name }
			type="number"
			inputmode="numeric"
			class="input validator peer w-full"
			value={ value }
			min="0"
			max="9999"
			step="1"
			required?={ required }
		/>
		<p class="validator-hint hidden peer-user-invalid:block text-hint">Enter a number from 0 to 9999.</p>
	</div>
}

// selectField is the same .fieldset / .fieldset-legend shell as the two above,
// with DaisyUI's .select in place of .input. Both are 2.5rem tall and read the
// same --input-color, so a picker sits on the same baseline as the text field
// beside it in the Identity grid.
//
// DaisyUI draws the chevron itself, as two linear-gradients in background-image
// pinned to the trailing edge, and reserves 1.75rem of end padding for them. So
// the <i class="selector"> SVG select.js used to append, and the absolute
// positioning that placed it, are both gone -- and with them the reason
// select.css needed position:relative on the wrapper.
//
// No .validator here, unlike the text and number fields. `required` on a <select>
// is only violated when the selected option's value is empty, and neither set
// has such an entry: the browser lands on the first one, so these two can
// never be :user-invalid. select.js's validate() had the same blind spot (it
// tested value === "" against a value that was never empty). A .validator-hint
// under these would be markup that can never reveal itself, so it is not
// rendered. `required` stays on the element as the honest declaration it is.
//
// w-full for the same reason as the inputs: .select is clamp(3rem, 20rem, 100%).
templ selectField(name string, label string, options []Option, value string, required bool) {
	<div class="fieldset">
		<label class="fieldset-legend" for={ name }>{ label }</label>
		<select
			id={ name }
			name={ name }
			class="select w-full"
			required?={ required }
		>
			for _, option := range options {
				<option value={ option.Value } selected?={ option.Value == value }>{ option.Label }</option>
			}
		</select>
	</div>
}
